WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:04.471
NARRATOR: The West
Bank of the River Nile,

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:08.074
home to the world's
most iconic monuments,

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:11.177
the mighty pyramids of Giza.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:15.382
The pyramids once housed
the bodies of the pharaohs.

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:18.385
But though ancient Egyptian
civilization lasted for nearly

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:22.956
3,000 years, its kings
only built huge tombs

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:26.292
like these for a few centuries.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.428
Egyptologists are
still trying to piece

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.797
together why the
pharaohs stopped

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:34.401
constructing giant pyramids.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:37.570
For Egyptologist Chris
Norton, the majesty

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:39.606
of the ancient
structures makes the fact

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:45.645
that Egyptians gave up building
them all the more incredible.

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:50.617
10 miles south of the legendary
pyramids of Giza is Saqqara.

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:52.285
CHRIS NAUNTON: When we
think about pyramids,

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:55.321
we tend to think of Giza, I
think, and the Great Pyramid

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:56.589
of Khufu in particular.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:58.725
But actually, this is
where it all began.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:01.628
NARRATOR: Chris has come to the
birthplace of pyramid building

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:06.199
to search for clues to why
Egyptians built giant pyramids

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:09.302
for less than 500 years.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:13.640
Constructed a century before
the iconic pyramids at Giza,

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:18.044
Egypt's first pyramid is
a 200 foot tall mausoleum

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:21.748
of six huge limestone
platforms carefully

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:25.285
engineered to spread the weight
of rock and prevent collapse.

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:36.396
Deep inside is a giant shaft
26 feet wide and 82 feet deep.

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:39.732
At the bottom, the intended
final resting place

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:41.801
of the Pharaoh Djoser.

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.770
CHRIS NAUNTON: Ultimately
that's what it's all about.

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:46.339
That's where the body of a king
is going to rest in eternity.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:47.640
So you've gone to
all this trouble

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:49.442
to create this
incredible monument

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:52.278
around the body of that person.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.414
It's pretty amazing.

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:57.450
NARRATOR: To house his
mummy, huge chunks of granite

00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.719
were slid down a
passage into the shaft

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:02.422
and stacked, creating
a giant sarcophagus

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:07.026
19 feet long and 11 feet high.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:11.231
CHRIS NAUNTON: My god,
these pieces are huge.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:15.134
Wow, it's amazing.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.936
NARRATOR: But this
wasn't just a tomb

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:22.075
designed to secure the pharaoh's
physical body for eternity.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:24.511
Crucially, for success
in the afterlife,

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.547
the pyramid ensured the king
was remembered by the living.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:35.121
Completed around
2,650 BC, it sparked

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:36.856
an architectural revolution.

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:44.364
Djoser's six tier giant
wasn't just the first pyramid.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.900
It was the world's first
monumental structure

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:48.868
built in stone.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:52.572
Over the next century, Egypt's
kings develop the concept,

00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:56.543
building monumental tombs all
along the Nile's West Bank,

00:02:56.609 --> 00:03:00.013
including the first
geometrically true pyramid,

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.282
the Red Pyramid,
and the misshapen

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:06.452
experiment, the Bent Pyramid.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:10.089
Then a dynasty of pharaohs
built the most iconic monuments

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:14.160
in Egypt, the pyramids of Giza.

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.996
But just a few short centuries
after the Great Pyramid

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:22.468
of Khufu rose from the desert,
a new era was on the horizon.

